[THOUGHT] I have systematically addressed all eight evidence domains:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — positional sole tingling during leg elevation with exercise;  
2) Course — reproducible, rapid onset/offset, no progression noted;  
3) Associated symptoms — none reported (no pain, weakness, bowel/bladder changes);  
4) Past medical history — 8-year type 2 diabetes, no surgeries or systemic illnesses;  
5) Medications & allergies — uncertain, but HbA1c confirms suboptimal control;  
6) Social/exposure — no occupational or activity triggers identified;  
7) Targeted physical exam — attempted sensory and provocative testing, though results not returned;  
8) Laboratory — HbA1c = 7.2% obtained, confirming metabolic contributor to neuropathy risk.  

The most specific, unifying diagnosis consistent with *sole-limited*, *exertional*, *positionally triggered* paresthesia in a diabetic patient is **tarsal tunnel syndrome**, a compressive mononeuropathy of the tibial nerve at the ankle. Alternative considerations — such as L5/S1 radiculopathy, peripheral polyneuropathy, or vascular claudication — are less compatible with the precise anatomical distribution and trigger pattern. No further diagnostic turns are justified within the 16-turn limit.

[FINISH]

